From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: do not swallow a TZ refusal of set_remote_state

preset_limit put the msm8998 wedge on a single write: 0x00000003 to
base+0x80124, the first VBIF register, from venus_set_registers(). Skipping the
preset table avoids the wedge entirely and the probe then fails cleanly with
-110 out of venus_boot_core()'s poll instead.

One cause explains both. If the venus CPU never leaves reset then VBIF is not
reachable -- so the first write there hangs the NoC -- and the firmware never
runs, so the readiness poll times out. On the TZ path releasing that CPU is
qcom_scm_set_remote_state()'s job, and this function currently maps its -EINVAL
to success. A TZ that does not implement the call therefore reports success
while venus stays held in reset, and nothing anywhere says so.

hw_state_debug=1 logs what TZ actually returned, which is the measurement.
hw_state_fallback=1 then takes the no-TZ path's action -- venus_reset_cpu(),
which programs the FW/CPA/NONPIX windows and brings the ARM9 out of reset --
rather than ignoring the refusal.

Both default off. If the fallback turns out to be what msm8998 needs, the right
patch is to stop special-casing -EINVAL rather than to add a knob.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/firmware.c | 37 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/firmware.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/firmware.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/firmware.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/firmware.c
@@ -53,14 +53,49 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Bring-up bisect, fifth stage. preset_limit put the msm8998 wedge on the first
+ * VBIF write, base+0x80124, in venus_set_registers(); with the preset table
+ * skipped nothing wedges and the probe instead fails -110 from
+ * venus_boot_core()'s poll. Both symptoms fall out of one thing: the venus CPU
+ * never actually leaving reset.
+ *
+ * On the TZ path that is qcom_scm_set_remote_state()'s job, and this function
+ * swallows -EINVAL from it -- so a TZ that does not implement the call reports
+ * success while venus stays held in reset, VBIF is unreachable, and the
+ * firmware never runs.
+ *
+ * hw_state_debug=1 prints what TZ actually returned. hw_state_fallback=1 then
+ * does what the no-TZ path does -- venus_reset_cpu() -- instead of swallowing
+ * it. Both default off, so this is inert unless asked for.
+ */
+static int hw_state_debug;
+module_param(hw_state_debug, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(hw_state_debug, "log what qcom_scm_set_remote_state() returns");
+
+static int hw_state_fallback;
+module_param(hw_state_fallback, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(hw_state_fallback,
+	"on TZ -EINVAL at resume, release the venus CPU directly instead of ignoring it");
+
 int venus_set_hw_state(struct venus_core *core, bool resume)
 {
 	int ret;
 
 	if (core->use_tz) {
 		ret = qcom_scm_set_remote_state(resume, 0);
-		if (resume && ret == -EINVAL)
+		if (hw_state_debug)
+			dev_info(core->dev,
+				 "venus: scm_set_remote_state(resume=%d) = %d\n",
+				 resume, ret);
+		if (resume && ret == -EINVAL) {
+			if (hw_state_fallback) {
+				dev_info(core->dev,
+					 "venus: TZ refused; releasing the CPU directly\n");
+				venus_reset_cpu(core);
+			}
 			ret = 0;
+		}
 		return ret;
 	}
 
